  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1153 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nGoing into Friday, the combined Panhandles are progged to fall \nunder southwest flow aloft with the next upper level trough \ndeepening over the Intermountain West down into the southern tips \nof CA/NV. The southwest flow is expected to increase the cloud \ncover limiting daytime highs a few degrees into the mid to upper \n70s for Fri. PoPs are expected to start to increase from west to \neast overnight Fri night as plenty of Pacific moisture gets \ncarried up in the southwest flow. PWATs are expected to begin \nincreasing Fri night to around an inch on Sat, maybe even \nslightly higher. Models have the system slowing down, and mainly \nconfining PoPs to the central to western combined Panhandles Fri \nnight into Sat, eventually spreading likely PoPs to include the \neastern Panhandles Sat night into Sun night. There is a bit of \nuncertainty to the timing of when the system will enter and exit \nthe combined Panhandles. Which will impact the overall potential \nfor rain amounts. Guidance is pointing to a potential excessive \nrainfall event at this time. In fact, WPC currently has the \ncentral to western parts of the combined Panhandles under a 15% \nchance for rainfall to exceed flash flood guidance Sat into Sat \nnight. With the eastern Panhandles under a 5% probability within \n25 mile of a given point. At this time wide spread severe storms \nare not anticipated at this time. \n\nTemperatures will be on a downward trend this weekend with \nwidespread lower 60s for an afternoon high Sun into Mon. These \ntemperatures are looking to be kept cool due to the rain and \ncloud cover, more so than any actual CAA behind a front. Winds are\nlooking to shift behind a front on Monday, however, temperatures\nare not looking to be much different than Sun ahead of the front.",
